Right. The distinction is calibrated trust, not blind obedience.

A demonstrated record of competence changes how much weight people reasonably give someone’s judgment. It creates a prior: this person has shown they can see farther, decide under pressure, and carry consequences. Good followers use that signal, but they do not surrender judgment entirely.

The healthier model is:

aptitude earns deference → deference buys speed → judgment remains distributed → refusal stays available when the order, logic, or consequences cross a real line.

That is much closer to mature military leadership than “rank means correctness.” The best teams work because leaders trust followers to exercise judgment, and followers trust leaders enough that every unusual move does not require a courtroom argument before execution.

So what they are really trusting is not infallibility. They are trusting capability, intent, accountability, and the leader’s willingness to be corrected when reality disagrees. That makes refusal part of loyalty rather than its opposite.